<main>
<section class="hero-dark page-hero"><div class="wrap"><span class="eyebrow">Optical operations guide</span><h1>Treat optical inventory as part of the patient workflow, not a separate stockroom.</h1><p>Frames and lenses move through purchasing, receiving, patient selection, orders, remakes, dispensing and financial reporting. The best inventory process keeps those steps connected to the patient and practice record.</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="btn-grad" href="https://taxrescrm.app/book?product=oculivo">Request a private demo&nbsp; →</a><a class="btn-outline" href="/solutions/optometry/">Explore optometry workflows</a></div></div></section>
<section class="section-light"><div class="wrap article-wrap">
<div class="quick"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Every frame should have a reliable brand, model, color, size, vendor, cost and retail context. Clean catalog data makes receiving, inventory counts, reorder decisions and sales reporting substantially easier.</div>
<section class="article-section"><h2>Use a consistent frame and product catalog</h2><p>Every frame should have a reliable brand, model, color, size, vendor, cost and retail context. Clean catalog data makes receiving, inventory counts, reorder decisions and sales reporting substantially easier.</p></section><section class="article-section"><h2>Track inventory movement, not just quantity on hand</h2><p>A count alone does not explain whether a frame was received, sold, reserved, returned, damaged or adjusted. Inventory movement should create a readable history that explains why the quantity changed.</p></section><section class="article-section"><h2>Connect optical orders to the patient</h2><p>The patient record should show the selected frame, lens order, lab or vendor, order status, balances, remakes and dispensing history so the optical team does not have to reconcile separate systems.</p></section><section class="article-section"><h2>Use reorder signals that reflect actual demand</h2><p>Reorder points work best when the practice can see sales velocity, vendor lead times, preferred brands and stock concentration. The goal is fewer dead frames and fewer missed sales because a proven style was unavailable.</p></section><section class="article-section"><h2>Measure margin and aging</h2><p>Practice leadership should be able to see inventory value, aging stock, sell-through, cost, retail value and margin by brand or category without building a separate spreadsheet.</p></section>
<section class="faq-block"><span class="eyebrow">FAQ</span><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><article><h3>What should optical inventory software track?</h3><p>Frames, SKUs, brand, model, color, size, cost, retail price, vendor, quantity, status, patient allocation, receiving and movement history.</p></article><article><h3>Why connect optical inventory to the patient record?</h3><p>It keeps product selection, orders, remakes, payments and dispensing attached to the same patient history used by the rest of the practice.</p></article><article><h3>What is inventory aging?</h3><p>Inventory aging measures how long items have remained in stock. It helps identify slow-moving product and purchasing patterns that may be tying up cash.</p></article></section>
</div></section>
